-- allium: 3
-- claims.allium
--
-- Scope: The fact store at the heart of claimgraph — every fact a reified
-- edge carrying valid time, transaction time, confidence, epistemic class,
-- source type, scope, and provenance. This spec owns the write path (the
-- assert decision: reinforce / insert / supersede / flag, with the trust
-- defenses), disuse decay as a read-time view, manual invalidation, and the
-- predicate registry lifecycle (curated core vocabulary, x/* staging,
-- promotion, deprecation).
--
-- Includes: Fact, Predicate; the assert decision tree; exclusion groups;
--   reinforcement; effective (decayed) confidence; invalidation; the
--   open-conflicts view; predicate coinage, promotion and deprecation.
-- Excludes:
--   - Entity identity, resolution and curation (entities.allium; this spec
--     consumes already-resolved GraphEntity references)
--   - Episodes, evidence, and the ingestion tiers that feed AssertFact
--     (ingestion.allium)
--   - The LLM judge, sweep, consolidation, compiled context, and outcome
--     reinforcement (maintenance.allium)
--   - Multi-writer oplogs, the write lease, reconcile, dump/load
--     (replication.allium)
--   - The storage engine, CLI/JSON wire shapes, and the MCP front-end
--     (implementation; every front-end drives this same behaviour)
--
-- Conventions carried across the whole system:
--   - Nothing is ever hard-deleted: a contradiction closes a validity
--     interval; the predecessor stays queryable in history.
--   - Bi-temporal, modeled not engine-native: valid time is settable on
--     BOTH ends of every write ("true Jan through March" is one fact);
--     recorded_at (transaction time) is append-only. Correction history of
--     beliefs about the past (XTDB-style) is deliberately out of scope.
--   - Objects are entities or literals (RDF-style): traversal only follows
--     entity objects; preferences live as literal facts without minting
--     junk nodes.
--   - Inverse directions are computed at query time, never stored as twin
--     facts — nothing to keep consistent on invalidation.
--   - The default scope is "project"; scope "external" marks facts about
--     things outside the repo (unresolvable imports, other systems).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Whoever drives a front-end: the human at the CLI, the coding agent via
-- the skill, or an ingestion tier acting on their behalf.
external entity Agent {
    name: String
}

-- A node in the graph. Governed by entities.allium: resolution (exact >
-- alias > unique normalized, type-guarded, self-healing), ambiguity
-- refusal, and the curation verbs live there. AssertFact receives subjects
-- and entity objects already resolved-or-created.
external entity GraphEntity {
    name: String
    scope: String

    assertions: Fact with subject = this
    current_assertions: assertions where is_currently_valid
}

-- Provenance bundle for a batch of writes. Governed by ingestion.allium.
external entity Episode {
    ref: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What kind of thing a fact's object is. Entity objects are traversable
-- graph edges; literal objects are free text (preferences, versions,
-- lessons) that never mint a node.
enum ObjectKind { entity | literal }

-- What a predicate's registry row permits as object kind. Predicates
-- declaring `either` decide per fact: an explicit caller choice wins, else
-- entity when a matching entity already exists, else literal.
enum ObjectKindRule { entity_only | literal_only | either }

-- What a predicate's LITERAL objects are — the contract the admission
-- screen reads (ingestion.allium, decided 2026-08-06). `value`: a
-- comparable datum (a version, a status, a language) whose length past a
-- sentence means the extractor stuffed prose into a slot built for data.
-- `prose`: a lesson or rationale, sentences BY DESIGN — the failure tier's
-- own rule is "the lesson, not the diff", and a lesson that fits a value's
-- bound is a slogan. One flat length cap defined the prose class as junk:
-- the first curated store's rejections measured 252–606 chars, all on
-- lesson-bearing predicates, two of them over by 2 and 7 characters — not
-- pathology being caught, the tail of a natural distribution being shaved.
-- The registry is already the vocabulary's authority on what each
-- predicate's objects may be (object_kinds, cardinality); which bound
-- applies belongs to the same row, not to one number in the screen.
enum ObjectShape { value | prose }

-- The epistemic class drives conflict policy: observations and preferences
-- supersede cleanly; a commitment on either side of a conflict flags — a
-- human decision is never silently overwritten by new evidence.
enum EpistemicClass { observation | commitment | preference }

-- Where a fact came from. Ranked by trust and capped by ceiling (see the
-- black box functions): human decisions and mechanical code derivation at
-- the top, LLM extraction in the middle, agent inference at the bottom.
enum SourceType { decision_record | user_assertion | code | session_log | failure_report | agent_note | inferred }

-- Caller override of the epistemic-class-derived conflict policy.
enum ConflictOverride { supersede | flag | ignore }

-- WHY a validity interval closed, as structure. The reason sentence survives
-- beside it for humans, and nothing parses it — it used to be the only
-- record, and each producer phrased itself differently, so a reader matching
-- one phrasing silently missed every other producer's closures.
--
--   superseded           the write path closed it for a newer assertion
--                        (including an epistemic escalation)
--   judged_superseded    the judge ruled the newer fact its legitimate
--                        successor (maintenance.allium)
--   judged_duplicate     the judge ruled it a restatement of another
--   merge_duplicate      an entity merge left the same claim twice
--                        (entities.allium)
--   reconcile_duplicate  two writers made the same claim independently
--                        (replication.allium)
--   code_absent          the declaration is gone from the source it was
--                        derived from (ingestion.allium) — nothing took its
--                        place, so there is no successor
--   manual               a human closed it by hand
--
-- superseded and judged_superseded are the SUPERSESSION kinds: a successor
-- took this claim's place, which is what "what changed" is asking about
-- (maintenance.allium's compiled briefing selects on exactly those two).
-- Duplicate collapses are deliberately outside that subset — nothing changed,
-- one of two identical rows was retired, and rendering X -> X is noise.
--
-- The set is explicitly OPEN. A store outlives the build that wrote it, so a
-- dump or a foreign writer's log can carry a kind this reader has never heard
-- of; it is kept verbatim and simply matches nothing. Adding a kind is
-- additive and does NOT move the persisted-format version
-- (replication.allium), which is precisely why no reader may assume the set
-- it knows is the whole set.
enum InvalidationKind { superseded | judged_superseded | judged_duplicate | merge_duplicate | reconcile_duplicate | code_absent | manual }

-- Predicate lifecycle: core/* rows are born stable (curated seed); x/*
-- coinages are born testing and either earn promotion (a NEW stable core
-- row is minted; the staging row deprecates with a forwarding pointer) or
-- rot in staging.
enum PredicateStatus { testing | stable | deprecated }

enum PredicateCategory { structural | procedural | decision | provenance | experimental }

-- one_value: a new value for (subject, predicate) contradicts the current
-- one. many_values: values accumulate (e.g. depends-on).
enum Cardinality { one_value | many_values }

-- Exclusion groups widen conflict detection across predicates: rows in the
-- same group declare mutually-exclusive stances toward the same object.
-- prefers / decided-against share stance; supersedes / superseded-by share
-- revision. Groups are deliberately conservative — a false conflict nags
-- the human, so only clearly-opposed pairs are declared; everything
-- fuzzier goes to the judge sweep (maintenance.allium).
enum ExclusionGroup { stance | revision }

-- Marks a predicate whose multiple values on one subject tend to be
-- alternatives rather than accumulation (prefers) — the judge sweep's
-- first candidate generator.
enum ValueExclusivity { exclusive | accumulating }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A registry row: every predicate is a first-class queryable row in the
-- same store, with a maps_to anchor to an established standard (PROV-O /
-- SPDX / DOAP / Dublin Core). 23 curated core/* rows are seeded; unknown
-- predicates are refused with a did-you-mean suggestion — the cheap
-- defense against LLM-driven predicate proliferation.
entity Predicate {
    name: String                     -- namespaced: "core/depends-on", "x/uses-pattern"
    label: String
    definition: String
    category: PredicateCategory
    object_kinds: ObjectKindRule
    object_shape: ObjectShape        -- default value. A row written before
                                     -- this field existed reads as the
                                     -- shipped seed's declaration for its
                                     -- name, else value — additive, no
                                     -- format bump, no store migration:
                                     -- the seed IS the authority core/*
                                     -- rows materialize
    cardinality: Cardinality
    default_epistemic: EpistemicClass?
    value_exclusivity: ValueExclusivity?
    exclusion_group: ExclusionGroup?
    inverse_of: Predicate?           -- documentation of the pair; inverses are
                                     -- computed at query time, never stored
    maps_to: String?                 -- anchor to the established standard
    status: PredicateStatus
    replaced_by: Predicate when status = deprecated

    transitions status {
        testing -> deprecated
        terminal: stable, deprecated
    }
}

-- The reified edge. Immutable in meaning once recorded: later writes may
-- close its validity interval, reset its reinforcement clock, raise its
-- ceiling-capped base confidence, or link conflicts — never rewrite what
-- it claimed.
entity Fact {
    subject: GraphEntity
    predicate: Predicate
    object_kind: ObjectKind
    object_entity: GraphEntity?      -- exactly one of these two is set,
    object_literal: String?          -- per object_kind (see ObjectMatchesKind)

    t_valid: Timestamp               -- valid time: when it became true
    t_invalid: Timestamp?            -- valid time: when it stopped; open when null
    recorded_at: Timestamp           -- transaction time: append-only
    last_reinforced_at: Timestamp    -- the disuse clock's anchor

    confidence: Decimal              -- the stored BASE; reads decay it (below)
    epistemic: EpistemicClass
    scope: String
    source_type: SourceType
    episode: Episode?                -- provenance: which ingestion produced it

    conflicts_with: Set<Fact>        -- links live on the NEWER fact of each pair

    -- WHY this interval closed, written when it closes. The kind is what a
    -- reader selects on; the sentence survives beside it for humans and
    -- nothing parses it; the successor is the claim that took this one's
    -- place. A successor is recorded for duplicate collapses too, not only
    -- supersessions — a row retired against no named counterpart reads as
    -- deleted for no reason a year later — and is absent only when nothing
    -- took the claim's place (code_absent, manual).
    invalidation_kind: InvalidationKind?
    invalidation_reason: String?
    successor: Fact?                 -- a plain fact id, deliberately NOT a
                                     -- resolved reference: a dump restores in
                                     -- file order and a foreign log replays in
                                     -- clock order, so the successor routinely
                                     -- does not exist yet when the predecessor
                                     -- closes. An id that resolves to nothing
                                     -- degrades to "no successor known" on
                                     -- read; it never refuses the write

    is_currently_valid: t_valid <= now and (t_invalid = null or t_invalid > now)

    -- Disuse decay is a VIEW, never stored and never a job: the base halves
    -- per half-life since last reinforcement, floored at
    -- config.confidence_floor. Commitments and decision-record facts never
    -- fade. as-of reads evaluate this at the queried instant, so time
    -- travel sees period-appropriate decay.
    effective_confidence: if epistemic = commitment or source_type = decision_record: confidence else: decayed(confidence, last_reinforced_at, now)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    default_confidence: Decimal = 0.8    -- when the caller supplies none
    half_life: Duration = 90.days        -- disuse decay half-life
    confidence_floor: Decimal = 0.05     -- decay never goes below this
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Black box functions (pure, deterministic):
--
--   decayed(base, anchor, at)      base * 0.5 ^ (days between anchor and at
--                                  / config.half_life), floored at
--                                  config.confidence_floor
--   trusted(source_type)           true for the top trust tier —
--                                  decision_record, user_assertion, code
--                                  (an absent source_type defaults to
--                                  user_assertion). The full ranking:
--                                  3: decision_record, user_assertion, code
--                                  2: session_log, failure_report
--                                  1: agent_note, inferred
--   outranks(a, b)                 source a sits strictly above source b in
--                                  that ranking (an absent b defaults to
--                                  user_assertion). Drives the defense: a
--                                  weaker source never silently supersedes
--                                  a stronger one.
--   ceiling(source_type)           the most a fact from this source may ever
--                                  claim: decision_record 1.0, code 0.95,
--                                  user_assertion 0.9, session_log 0.7,
--                                  failure_report 0.7, agent_note 0.65,
--                                  inferred 0.6, and 0.9 for
--                                  a source the table does not list — a fact
--                                  re-derived 500 times stays distinguishable
--                                  from a human decision. BOTH ends of a
--                                  fact's life are capped by it: birth (see
--                                  AssertFact's `base`) and reinforcement.
--                                  Birth has to be capped because nothing
--                                  later brings a fact back down.
--   reinforced(base, incoming, source_type)
--                                  the new base after a re-assertion:
--                                  max(base, min(ceiling(source_type),
--                                  incoming)) — never lowered by weaker
--                                  evidence, raised only up to the fact's
--                                  source ceiling as it stands AFTER any
--                                  re-sourcing (decided 2026-07-26: a
--                                  higher-ceiling incoming source upgrades
--                                  the fact's source_type first — see the
--                                  reinforcement branch), and a base
--                                  already above its ceiling is preserved,
--                                  not clawed back.
--   resolved_class(explicit, pred) caller's class > the predicate's
--                                  default_epistemic > observation
--   stronger(stated, held)         the class the caller STATED binds harder
--                                  than the one the standing fact holds, in
--                                  the order observation < preference <
--                                  commitment. Keyed off what was stated and
--                                  nothing else: false when the caller stated
--                                  no class at all, because resolved_class
--                                  fills in the predicate's default and
--                                  comparing RESOLVED values would make every
--                                  code or notes pass escalate the facts it
--                                  wrote last time, unboundedly and silently.
--   resolved_kind(explicit, pred, object)
--                                  the object kind per the registry row:
--                                  entity_only / literal_only are forced
--                                  (an explicit mismatch is refused);
--                                  either takes the explicit choice, else
--                                  entity when a GraphEntity of that name
--                                  exists, else literal
--   filter(facts, p)               facts satisfying p
--   find_duplicate(facts, kind, object)
--                                  the fact among these whose object is
--                                  the same one (entity identity for
--                                  entity objects, string equality for
--                                  literals), or null
--   different_valued(facts, kind, object)
--                                  the complement: facts whose object is a
--                                  DIFFERENT one — the rivals a one_value
--                                  predicate contradicts
--   loosely_same_object(fact, object)
--                                  the same object across the entity /
--                                  literal divide: normalized
--                                  name-or-literal equality, so
--                                  decided-against "GraphQL" collides with
--                                  prefers GraphQL. Over-matching is safe —
--                                  it produces a flag the judge can rule
--                                  compatible.
--   is_experimental(name)          the name is in the x/* staging namespace
--   revenant_of(subject, pred, object)
--                                  this (subject, predicate) already lived
--                                  through this object value and closed
--                                  its interval — the value is a revenant

-- THE write path. Every fact from every tier — direct assert, batch
-- ingest, code analysis, session extraction, notes, failures, ADRs
-- (reconcile has its own replay path) — arrives here and gets the same
-- treatment.
--
-- The decision, in order:
--   1. An exact duplicate of a currently-valid fact REINFORCES it: the
--      world (or the user) just confirmed the fact, so its disuse clock
--      resets and its base may rise toward the source ceiling — never
--      above it, never down, and never by repetition alone. Stronger
--      evidence re-sources first: an incoming source whose ceiling is
--      higher upgrades the fact's source_type, and the raised ceiling
--      applies from that reinforcement on. With one
--      exception: a duplicate re-asserted with an explicitly STATED class
--      stronger than the standing fact's is an ESCALATION, and escalation
--      supersedes rather than reinforces (see the branch).
--   2. A conflicting set is gathered: for one_value predicates, the other
--      currently-valid values of (subject, predicate); plus, for any
--      predicate in an exclusion group, currently-valid facts on the
--      subject's group-mate predicates aimed at loosely the same object.
--      If non-empty, the policy decides (see the branches below).
--   3. With no conflict, a low-trust source re-asserting a value this
--      (subject, predicate) already lived through and invalidated — a
--      revenant, either stale or adversarial — flags against the live
--      rivals instead of quietly coexisting.
--   4. Otherwise the fact inserts cleanly.
--
-- An unknown x/* predicate auto-registers on first use (status testing);
-- an unknown core/* or un-namespaced predicate is refused with a
-- did-you-mean suggestion. A deprecated predicate is refused with its
-- replaced_by forwarding address.
rule AssertFact {
    when: AssertFact(agent, subject, predicate, object, object_kind?,
                     epistemic?, confidence?, scope?, source_type?, episode?,
                     on_conflict?, valid_from?, valid_until?)

    let pred = Predicate{name: predicate}

    requires: exists pred or is_experimental(predicate)
    requires: not exists pred or pred.status != deprecated
    -- A closed valid-time interval must run forward; an inverted one is
    -- refused, never silently fixed.
    requires: valid_until = null or valid_until > (valid_from ?? now)

    let effective_at = valid_from ?? now
    let overridden = on_conflict != null
    let source = source_type ?? user_assertion
    -- A fact cannot be BORN above the trust its own source declares — the
    -- default confidence included. The ingestion tiers already clamp their
    -- own candidates, so this closes the direct-assert hole behind them, and
    -- it has to close at birth: reinforcement is a high-water mark that never
    -- claws a base back down, so a fact minted above its ceiling sits above
    -- it permanently and the trust model is defeated for that row.
    -- Range before ceiling (decided 2026-07-26): the stated confidence is
    -- clamped into [0,1] first — a negative or >1 value is a caller bug,
    -- silently corrected the same way the ceiling clamp corrects
    -- over-claiming, and the top stops depending on the accident that the
    -- highest ceiling is 1.0.
    let base = min(ceiling(source), max(0.0, min(1.0, confidence ?? config.default_confidence)))

    ensures:
        if not exists pred:
            -- First use of an x/* coinage: auto-registered at testing
            -- status, object kinds open, values accumulating — so a fresh
            -- coinage cannot conflict, and its fact inserts cleanly.
            let coined = Predicate.created(
                name: predicate,
                label: predicate,
                definition: "auto-registered on first use; promote to core/* once proven",
                category: experimental,
                object_kinds: either,
                object_shape: value,     -- conservative until a human
                                         -- registration says otherwise: a
                                         -- coinage has no declared contract,
                                         -- and prose admission is earned by
                                         -- declaration, not by length
                cardinality: many_values,
                status: testing
            )
            Fact.created(
                subject: subject, predicate: coined,
                object_kind: resolved_kind(object_kind, coined, object),
                t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                confidence: base,
                epistemic: resolved_class(epistemic, coined),
                scope: scope ?? "project",
                source_type: source,
                episode: episode, conflicts_with: {}
            )
        else:
            let klass = resolved_class(epistemic, pred)
            let kind = resolved_kind(object_kind, pred, object)
            let existing = filter(subject.current_assertions, f => f.predicate = pred)
            let duplicate = find_duplicate(existing, kind, object)
            let antagonists = filter(subject.current_assertions,
                                     f => f.predicate != pred
                                          and f.predicate.exclusion_group != null
                                          and f.predicate.exclusion_group = pred.exclusion_group
                                          and loosely_same_object(f, object))
            let rivals = if pred.cardinality = one_value: different_valued(existing, kind, object) else: {}
            let conflicting = rivals + antagonists

            if duplicate != null and stronger(epistemic, duplicate.epistemic):
                -- Epistemic escalation. The caller did not restate the claim,
                -- they raised what it BINDS as — "this isn't just something we
                -- observed, we decided it" — and reinforcement would carry the
                -- old class forward and report the escalation as recorded. The
                -- difference is not cosmetic: an observation supersedes
                -- silently on the next contradiction and fades by disuse,
                -- while a commitment flags for a human and never decays.
                -- Superseding rather than mutating the standing row is what
                -- makes the history read observation-then-commitment.
                if duplicate.t_valid > effective_at:
                    -- A backdated escalation flags for the same reason a
                    -- backdated supersede does: closing the predecessor at a
                    -- valid-from earlier than its own leaves it valid at no
                    -- instant at all.
                    let escalation_flag = Fact.created(
                        subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                        t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                        recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                        confidence: base,
                        epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                        source_type: source,
                        episode: episode, conflicts_with: {duplicate}
                    )
                    ConflictFlagged(fact: escalation_flag, reason: "backdated-overlap")
                else:
                    let escalated = Fact.created(
                        subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                        t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                        recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                        confidence: base,
                        epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                        source_type: source,
                        episode: episode, conflicts_with: {}
                    )
                    duplicate.t_invalid = effective_at
                    duplicate.invalidation_kind = superseded
                    duplicate.successor = escalated
                    duplicate.invalidation_reason = "superseded by <successor>"
            else if duplicate != null:
                -- Reinforcement: clock reset is the mechanism; base is a
                -- ceiling-capped high-water mark. A weaker or equal stated
                -- class reinforces like any other restatement, and an unstated
                -- one changes nothing even on a predicate whose registry
                -- default is commitment.
                -- Re-sourcing (decided 2026-07-26): stronger evidence may
                -- promote the row it confirms. An incoming source whose
                -- ceiling is higher than the standing one's re-sources the
                -- fact before the base moves — a decision record
                -- re-asserting a code-derived fact lifts it toward 1.0; a
                -- human re-asserting a session extraction lifts it out of
                -- the 0.7 band. Both assertions' episodes stay in history,
                -- so the promotion is auditable.
                if ceiling(source) > ceiling(duplicate.source_type):
                    duplicate.source_type = source
                duplicate.last_reinforced_at = now
                duplicate.confidence = reinforced(duplicate.confidence, confidence, duplicate.source_type)
                FactReinforced(fact: duplicate)
            else if conflicting.count > 0 and overridden and on_conflict = ignore:
                -- Caller opted out of the machinery entirely: plain
                -- insert, values coexist unlinked.
                Fact.created(
                    subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                    t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                    recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                    confidence: base,
                    epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                    source_type: source,
                    episode: episode, conflicts_with: {}
                )
            else if conflicting.count > 0:
                let wants_flag = (overridden and on_conflict = flag)
                                 or (not overridden
                                     and (klass = commitment
                                          or conflicting.any(f => f.epistemic = commitment)))
                let backdated = conflicting.any(f => f.t_valid > effective_at)
                let outranked = conflicting.any(f => outranks(f.source_type, source_type))
                if wants_flag or backdated or outranked:
                    -- Flag: both sides stay valid, the conflict is linked
                    -- (on the newer fact) and surfaces for review. Three
                    -- roads in:
                    --   * a commitment on either side (or the caller
                    --     asked) — human decisions are never silently
                    --     overwritten;
                    --   * backdated overlap — a successor starting
                    --     strictly BEFORE a predecessor is a valid-time
                    --     contradiction, not a handoff; it is never
                    --     silently inverted, even under an explicit
                    --     supersede override;
                    --   * outranked — a lower-trust source never silently
                    --     closes a higher-trust fact's interval.
                    let flagged = Fact.created(
                        subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                        t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                        recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                        confidence: base,
                        epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                        source_type: source,
                        episode: episode, conflicts_with: conflicting
                    )
                    if wants_flag:
                        ConflictFlagged(fact: flagged, reason: "policy")
                    else if backdated:
                        ConflictFlagged(fact: flagged, reason: "backdated-overlap")
                    else:
                        ConflictFlagged(fact: flagged, reason: "outranked")
                else:
                    -- Clean succession: each predecessor's interval closes
                    -- at the successor's valid-from, so the new truth
                    -- begins exactly where the old one ends and an as-of
                    -- query between two versions returns exactly one.
                    -- Equal starts leave the predecessor an empty
                    -- interval — immediately replaced, never observably
                    -- valid. Non-lossy: the predecessor stays in history.
                    let successor = Fact.created(
                        subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                        t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                        recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                        confidence: base,
                        epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                        source_type: source,
                        episode: episode, conflicts_with: {}
                    )
                    for f in conflicting:
                        f.t_invalid = effective_at
                        f.invalidation_kind = superseded
                        f.successor = successor
                        f.invalidation_reason = "superseded by <successor>"
            else if not trusted(source_type)
                    and revenant_of(subject, pred, object)
                    and existing.count > 0
                    and (not overridden or (on_conflict != ignore and on_conflict != supersede)):
                -- The revenant defense: a non-trusted source re-asserting
                -- a dead value is either stale or adversarial — it flags
                -- against the currently-live rivals instead of quietly
                -- coexisting. Overridable, like everything here, with an
                -- explicit on_conflict.
                let revenant = Fact.created(
                    subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                    t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                    recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                    confidence: base,
                    epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                    source_type: source,
                    episode: episode, conflicts_with: existing
                )
                ConflictFlagged(fact: revenant, reason: "revenant")
            else:
                Fact.created(
                    subject: subject, predicate: pred, object_kind: kind,
                    t_valid: effective_at, t_invalid: valid_until,
                    recorded_at: now, last_reinforced_at: now,
                    confidence: base,
                    epistemic: klass, scope: scope ?? "project",
                    source_type: source,
                    episode: episode, conflicts_with: {}
                )
}

-- Close a fact's validity interval by hand. The timestamp is the
-- VALID-time end — when it stopped being true, not merely when we noticed.
-- The kind is manual and there is no successor: a human closing a claim is
-- saying it stopped holding, not naming what replaced it. Whatever prose they
-- give stays prose — a reason reading "superseded by a better plan" is a
-- sentence, never a link, which is exactly what a reader parsing reasons
-- instead of kinds could not tell.
rule InvalidateFact {
    when: InvalidateFact(agent, fact, reason?, at?)
    requires: (at ?? now) >= fact.t_valid
    ensures:
        fact.t_invalid = at ?? now
        fact.invalidation_kind = manual
        fact.invalidation_reason = reason ?? "manually invalidated"
}

-- Explicit coinage (the CLI's predicates register): only x/* may be coined
-- at runtime; core/* is curated in the seed vocabulary.
rule RegisterPredicate {
    when: RegisterPredicate(agent, name, definition?, object_kinds?, object_shape?, cardinality?, default_epistemic?)
    requires: is_experimental(name)
    ensures: Predicate.created(
        name: name,
        label: name,
        definition: definition ?? "auto-registered on first use; promote to core/* once proven",
        category: experimental,
        object_kinds: object_kinds ?? either,
        object_shape: object_shape ?? value,
        cardinality: cardinality ?? many_values,
        default_epistemic: default_epistemic,
        status: testing
    )
}

-- Graduate a staging predicate: mint the stable core/* twin (fields
-- carried from the staging row, overridable), rewrite every fact onto it —
-- a rename of the TERM, not a belief change: validity and transaction time
-- are untouched — and deprecate the staging term with a forwarding
-- pointer, so further writes to it fail with the replacement's address.
-- Promotion mints a NEW core term; merging into an existing one is a
-- curation decision, not a promotion.
rule PromotePredicate {
    when: PromotePredicate(agent, staging, core_name)
    requires: staging.status = testing
    requires: not exists Predicate{name: core_name}
    ensures:
        let core = Predicate.created(
            name: core_name,
            label: staging.label,
            definition: staging.definition,
            category: staging.category,
            object_kinds: staging.object_kinds,
            object_shape: staging.object_shape,
            cardinality: staging.cardinality,
            status: stable
        )
        for f in Facts:
            if f.predicate = staging:
                f.predicate = core
        staging.status = deprecated
        staging.replaced_by = core
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Closed intervals never run backwards. Equality is permitted: an
-- equal-start supersession leaves the predecessor an empty interval
-- (immediately replaced, never observably valid) by design.
invariant IntervalRunsForward {
    for f in Facts:
        f.t_invalid != null implies f.t_invalid >= f.t_valid
}

invariant ObjectMatchesKind {
    for f in Facts:
        (f.object_kind = entity implies f.object_entity != null)
        and (f.object_kind = literal implies f.object_literal != null)
}

-- Conflict links live on the newer fact of each pair, so the open-conflicts
-- view can always present newer-vs-established.
invariant ConflictLinksPointBackward {
    for f in Facts:
        f.conflicts_with.all(c => c.recorded_at <= f.recorded_at)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The one behavioural surface every front-end shares: the CLI verbs, the
-- project skill, and the MCP tools (memory_assert and friends) all drive
-- exactly this contract. MCP is a latency optimization, not a second
-- behaviour.
surface ClaimStore {
    facing agent: Agent

    provides:
        AssertFact(agent, subject, predicate, object, object_kind?,
                   epistemic?, confidence?, scope?, source_type?, episode?,
                   on_conflict?, valid_from?, valid_until?)
        InvalidateFact(agent, fact, reason?, at?)
        RegisterPredicate(agent, name, definition?, object_kinds?, object_shape?, cardinality?, default_epistemic?)
        PromotePredicate(agent, staging, core_name)

    @guarantee NothingHardDeleted
        -- No operation removes a fact. Contradiction and reconciliation
        -- close validity intervals; history — including every closure's
        -- kind, reason and successor — remains queryable forever.

    @guarantee ClosuresCarryTheirReason
        -- Every interval this system closes records WHY as a kind, and
        -- WHICH claim took its place when one did. Selection is on the kind
        -- alone: the sentence beside it is for humans, no reader parses it,
        -- and so no producer can phrase itself out of a reader's selection
        -- the way each of them once did. Rows closed before the kinds
        -- existed carry none, and a reader that needs them says so rather
        -- than inventing one.

    @guarantee TimeTravel
        -- Reads accept an as-of instant and answer from facts valid at
        -- that instant, with decay evaluated at that instant. Between a
        -- supersession's two versions, exactly one fact is valid.

    @guarantee HistoryIsComplete
        -- The history of (subject, predicate) returns every version, valid
        -- and invalidated, time-ordered — the single best demonstration of
        -- why this beats a markdown pile.

    @guarantee DecayIsAView
        -- Effective confidence is computed at read time from the stored
        -- base and the reinforcement anchor; no batch job ever rewrites
        -- confidence. Minimum-confidence filters and search ranking use
        -- the effective value; invalidated facts never surface in
        -- current-truth reads.

    @guarantee UnknownPredicatesRefusedHelpfully
        -- An unknown non-x/* predicate is refused with the closest
        -- registered names attached (did-you-mean); a deprecated one is
        -- refused with its replaced_by forwarding address.

    @guarantee ConflictsSurfaceForHumans
        -- Open conflicts (flagged fact vs still-valid candidate) are
        -- always listable; flagged facts are excluded from the compiled
        -- current-truth view (maintenance.allium) until resolved.
}

